WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:01.735 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Our next guest
is a Golden Globe, DGA,

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:04.070
and BAFTA Award-winning director
for her film "Nomadland,"

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is currently
in theaters and on Hulu

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:08.742 size:84% position:42%
and has been nominated
for six Academy Awards.

00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:09.476 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Let's take a look.

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:12.078 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The way I see it
is that the Titanic is sinking

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:14.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, uh, economic times
are changing.

00:00:14.614 --> 00:00:18.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so my goal is to get the
life boats out

00:00:18.251 --> 00:00:20.854 align:start size:94% position:6%
and get as many people
into the life boats as I can

00:00:20.854 --> 00:00:22.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah. Yeah.
Nah, there's plenty.

00:00:22.856 --> 00:00:25.458 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah, we've got plenty,
jump and shout.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:26.760 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Let you know, huh?

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.228 align:start size:94% position:6%
-What's that?
-It's vegan.

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:29.029 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-This is vegan?

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.264 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, I'm a -- I'm a carnivore.
-Good?

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:32.065 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay.
-Thank you.

00:00:32.065 --> 00:00:34.234 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, okay. Thank you, though
Looks good.

00:00:34.234 --> 00:00:35.235 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Another carnivore?

00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:36.603 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She's a carnivore.
-Okay.

00:00:36.603 --> 00:00:39.172 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Please welcome to the sho
Chloe Zhao.

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:40.340 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
How are you, Chloe?

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:42.876 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm good. Hi.
Thanks for having me.

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:44.878 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, my God.
I'm delighted to have you.

00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:48.181
Congratulations both on
the awards you've been gathering

00:00:48.181 --> 00:00:52.052
as we go into Oscar season
as well as your six nominations.

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.353 line:92%
I imagine it was very surrea

00:00:53.353 --> 00:00:55.822 size:84% position:42%
for the film to receive
that many nominations.

00:00:55.822 --> 00:00:59.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you someone
who enjoys that on the day

00:00:59.125 --> 00:01:01.127 size:81% position:41% line:92%
when you get the news?

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:04.931 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm usually asleep when
those announcements happens.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:06.666 line:92%
and then -- No, I am happy

00:01:06.666 --> 00:01:10.904
And when we set up to make
a film like this the way we did,

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:12.739 line:92%
this is not what we expect. No

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:15.809 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I've also read
you talking about the fact

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:19.779
that it's extra special
because you have so many friends

00:01:19.779 --> 00:01:23.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
amongst those who've been
nominated this year.

00:01:23.817 --> 00:01:25.685 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. This year feels lik
it's going to be

00:01:25.685 --> 00:01:29.522
a really, really intimate
affair, which is exciting.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:31.658 align:start size:94% position:6%
There are people that are my
co-workers

00:01:31.658 --> 00:01:34.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
who were producers
on my previous films,

00:01:34.461 --> 00:01:37.297 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and classmates who I --

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:39.499 line:92%
We know each other way too well.

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:40.767 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughs ]

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:43.937
And -- And also, people
I admire for a long time,

00:01:43.937 --> 00:01:45.605 size:78% position:39%
people that influence
my career so much.

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:48.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so having all these people
in a room together

00:01:48.007 --> 00:01:48.842 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
is gonna be great.

00:01:48.842 --> 00:01:50.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Your part --
-And be with people.

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
And be with people.
-Yeah, to be with people.

00:01:52.512 --> 00:01:53.746 align:start size:94% position:6%
It doesn't even matter
who they are.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:54.881 align:start size:94% position:6%
You don't even
have to respect them.

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.783 align:start size:94% position:6%
If they're people,
it's a win right now.

00:01:56.783 --> 00:01:58.318 line:92%
-Exactly. A person! Hug me

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:01.187 align:start size:94% position:6%
Oh, no. We can't do, either.
No, not yet.

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:03.590 line:92%
-So, this is also very nice.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:06.292 align:start size:94% position:6%
Your partner was the
cinematographer on this film

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:08.328 align:start size:94% position:6%
and, so, you're living
under one roof.

00:02:08.328 --> 00:02:12.232
He also received his first
Academy Award nomination

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:13.800 size:84% position:42% line:92%
and very deservedly so.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.201 line:92%
This film is beautifully shot.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:16.669 align:start size:94% position:6%
That must have saved any
awkwardness

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:21.941 align:start size:94% position:6%
if you were the only one
who got the call that morning.

00:02:21.941 --> 00:02:24.244 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, that's
what pets are for.

00:02:24.244 --> 00:02:26.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, you can always
call the dogs over

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:27.080 size:78% position:39% line:92%
to avoid awkwardness.

00:02:27.080 --> 00:02:29.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, we're definitely
both super happy,

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:33.753
you know, more happy the other
person is nominated than us.

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:40.026
Out of love, yes, but mostly out
of avoiding awkwardness, yes

00:02:40.026 --> 00:02:41.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
-This is a --
For those who don't know,

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:43.096 align:start size:94% position:6%
this is a beautiful film
about people,

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:45.465 size:81% position:41%
nomads, for, you know,
lack of a better term,

00:02:45.465 --> 00:02:46.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
who don't have
a permanent address.

00:02:46.499 --> 00:02:49.969
And they're sort of findin
their way across the country

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:50.803 line:92%
This was based on a book.

00:02:50.803 --> 00:02:53.239 size:78% position:39%
I'm wondering how you
first got introduced

00:02:53.239 --> 00:02:56.776 align:start size:94% position:6%
to this piece of nonfictio
and how it --

00:02:56.776 --> 00:03:01.247 align:start size:94% position:6%
sort of what the journey was
to making it into a film.

00:03:01.247 --> 00:03:02.448 line:92%
-Well, Frances McDormand,

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.350 align:start size:94% position:6%
who's also one of the producer
on the film,

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:06.819 align:start size:94% position:6%
she and her producing partner,
Peter Spears,

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.289 align:start size:94% position:6%
optioned the book, "Nomadland"
by Jessica Bruder.

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:14.027
And Fran was in Toronto when she
was supposed to be doing press

00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
She sneaked away
and watched a movie

00:03:16.596 --> 00:03:18.998 align:start size:94% position:6%
and that was "The Rider,"
my -- my second film.

00:03:18.998 --> 00:03:22.068
And I think as a producer, she
sort of put two things together.

00:03:22.068 --> 00:03:23.670 align:start size:94% position:6%
She goes, oh, maybe there's
different way

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:26.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
of making this film
than the one she's used to

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:27.674 size:78% position:39% line:92%
And she called me up.

00:03:27.674 --> 00:03:29.442 align:start size:94% position:6%
-One of the things that's very
different about this film

00:03:29.442 --> 00:03:33.313
is it's no surprise to anyon
whose seen Frances McDormand

00:03:33.313 --> 00:03:34.647 line:92%
that she's wonderful in it

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:38.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you also cast
a lot of non-actors,

00:03:38.251 --> 00:03:41.721 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it brings a real
authenticity to the film.

00:03:41.721 --> 00:03:44.090 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is that something you knew
from the very beginning

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.059 line:92%
that you would use people --

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:48.461 align:start size:94% position:6%
many people who appeared
in the book that are playing

00:03:48.461 --> 00:03:50.697 size:84% position:42%
slightly fictionalized
versions of themselves?

00:03:50.697 --> 00:03:55.702
-Mm-hmm. Yeah. That's what
excites me about this film

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:58.438
It's when I read the book
and then it was from Fran,

00:03:58.438 --> 00:04:02.075
and I thought, "Can these
two things come together?"

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:05.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, to have these really
interesting characters,

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:09.882
that it's going to provide a
kind of undeniable authenticit

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you don't usually
see onscreen.

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:16.723
And yet they're united togethe
by a very strong performance

00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:18.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
with an emotional arc
that is going to be able

00:04:18.725 --> 00:04:22.829 align:start size:94% position:6%
to piece them together,
like by Frances McDormand.

00:04:22.829 --> 00:04:25.565 align:start size:94% position:6%
That was really
the identity of the film,

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:28.501 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I got excited about that
before anything else.

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:31.170 size:78% position:39%
-Do you find yourself
directing differently

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you're dealing with
accomplished actors on one han

00:04:34.007 --> 00:04:37.310
and then people who haven'
done film work in the past

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or do you speak to everybody
the same way?

00:04:39.512 --> 00:04:41.681
I mean, how do you get those
performances out of people

00:04:41.681 --> 00:04:45.585
who are, you know, ultimatel
you probably just want the

00:04:45.585 --> 00:04:47.754 line:92%
to be true to who they are

00:04:47.754 --> 00:04:50.923
-Yeah, I have to -- The most
important part is writing,

00:04:50.923 --> 00:04:53.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
writing for
non-professional actors,

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:57.830 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you want them
to be a version of themselves,

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.165 line:92%
and that's why you cast them

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:01.834 size:84% position:42%
And you don't want them
to be anything else.

00:05:01.834 --> 00:05:03.436 size:84% position:42% line:92%
But writing them and --

00:05:03.436 --> 00:05:07.407
So, by the time I got to set
the stuff they're going to say

00:05:07.407 --> 00:05:10.376 align:start size:94% position:6%
are mostly things
they already said to me.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:11.811 line:92%
And then they can take me

00:05:11.811 --> 00:05:14.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
to places that's going
to be surprising.

00:05:14.781 --> 00:05:16.249 line:92%
But with Fran, she would say

00:05:16.249 --> 00:05:17.750 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I probably pay
a lot more attention

00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:20.720 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the non-professional actors
than I do her.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.789 line:92%
I usually film her at the end.

00:05:22.789 --> 00:05:27.160
So poor Fran would get like,
you know, couple -- two takes.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:28.428 line:92%
Not that she needed more.

00:05:28.428 --> 00:05:29.862 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then sometimes
she would give me

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:32.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
like four or five
different reactions

00:05:32.865 --> 00:05:34.067 line:92%
just in case in the edit,

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:35.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I would want
to do things differently,

00:05:35.835 --> 00:05:39.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
because the non-professional
actors often surprised us

00:05:39.072 --> 00:05:40.573 line:92%
with things we didn't expect

00:05:40.573 --> 00:05:43.976
-As I've mentioned multipl
times now, it's a beautiful film

00:05:43.976 --> 00:05:46.346 align:start size:94% position:6%
that shows a lot of places
in this country

00:05:46.346 --> 00:05:48.348 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I'm sure many people
have never been.

00:05:48.348 --> 00:05:52.185 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are there places that you went
in the making of this movi

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:54.887 line:92%
that you want to return to

00:05:54.887 --> 00:05:57.657 line:92%
-Definitely Quartzsite, Arizona.

00:05:57.657 --> 00:05:58.591 line:92%
If you haven't been -- You know,

00:05:58.591 --> 00:06:00.593 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think because
it's so hot in the summer,

00:06:00.593 --> 00:06:05.198
so then every year around winter
time is when all the nomadic -

00:06:05.198 --> 00:06:08.701
People who live a nomadic
lifestyle over the country

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:09.836 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
gather together.

00:06:09.836 --> 00:06:11.237 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
It's quite a sight.

00:06:11.237 --> 00:06:13.139 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
I love that place.

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:14.440 line:92%
-I'd like to finish by askin

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:17.110
about another project you're
working on that's so exciting.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:20.113 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're also a member
of the Marvel Universe now

00:06:20.113 --> 00:06:23.816 align:start size:94% position:6%
and have "The Eternals"
coming out later this year

00:06:23.816 --> 00:06:26.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is comics something
that you were a fan of?

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:29.522 align:start size:94% position:6%
Were you aware of that world
before you got involved?

00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:33.259 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I grew up with manga more tha
the American comic books.

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.026 line:92%
It was the Japanese manga.

00:06:34.026 --> 00:06:38.664
But I have been a fan of the MCU
for about a decade now

00:06:38.664 --> 00:06:40.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
because of the movies.
And I think --

00:06:40.466 --> 00:06:42.135 size:84% position:42%
I don't know if that
helped my pitch or not,

00:06:42.135 --> 00:06:45.171 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think they could tel
that I was a fan.

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:47.740 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, it's a really
exciting marriage

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.543 size:84% position:42%
to think of you helming
something like that,

00:06:50.543 --> 00:06:51.944 line:92%
and I can't wait for that.

00:06:51.944 --> 00:06:53.179 line:92%
Best of luck at the Oscars

00:06:53.179 --> 00:06:56.649 size:84% position:42%
and thank you so much
for the beautiful film.

00:06:56.649 --> 00:06:58.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
Thank you for having me.

00:06:58.351 --> 00:06:58.885 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Of course.

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:01.487 size:88% position:44%
"Nomadland" is currently
in theaters and on Hulu.

